5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B2} 
Building on the points from colleagues 1, 4, 10, 13 and 14, B2 stands out because it simultaneously squeezes the liberties available to both the A2-C2 cluster and the B3-B4 stones while cutting their potential connection toward the edge. The surrounding white stones at B5, C3 and C4 already limit external breathing room, so any black reply (whether extending at A3/A4 or trying to reinforce at B1) leaves at least one subgroup with only two liberties that white can continue to fill in sequence. 

By contrast, moves like B1 or C5 appear to leave one of the black subgroups an extra escape option or connection that delays the capture, and the empty points raised by the {} group (A1, A3, A4) simply do not reduce both clusters at once. If the {} colleagues can clarify how black would answer a B2 play without eventually losing the B3-B4 stones, that would help test whether the simultaneous-pressure line truly forces the outcome.

