After re-examining the full liberty counts and connections, B2 remains the only move that simultaneously reduces both the A2-C1-C2 cluster and the B3-B4 group while cutting their remaining escape paths. Placing White there leaves Black with at most one shared liberty at B1; any reply (A3, A1, or B1) lets White continue filling liberties in sequence and capture at least one group on the next move. 

B1, by contrast, only ataris the C1-C2 stones and leaves the A2-B3-B4 stones with an extra liberty at B2, allowing Black to answer elsewhere and later capture the atari stone or connect out. The same holds for A3 or C5, which are too slow and permit Black to extend. Thus only B2 forces capture regardless of Black's replies.

