{B2} 

The position features three independent Black groups with the following liberties: {A2} (A1/A3/B2), {B3-B4} (A3/A4/B2), and {C1-C2} (B1/B2). White at B2 is the sole point that simultaneously fills a liberty of both {A2} and {C1-C2} while also reducing {B3-B4}, placing two groups in atari. Black has only one reply and can therefore save at most one group; White captures the other on the subsequent move, after which the remaining stones have neither eyes nor escape routes. 

B1 fails because it only ataris {C1-C2}; Black answers safely at B2 (capturing the single White stone after it fills the last liberty of B1) and thereby restores a liberty plus potential connection toward A1. All other candidates (A3, A1, C5, D3, …) leave at least one Black group an extra liberty or connection that can be used to live or run out. Hence only B2 initiates an unconditional capture sequence against optimal replies.

